	"_source": "Father's cutout rig (2026-11-20; docs/art/characters/FATHER.md). Canvas px of the 1024 × 1024 generated images. Legs split at the knee (thigh and shin) — his jeans show the whole leg, unlike grandma's skirt. Front: body = base_front, head / arms / legs cut from rig_front. Back: body = base_back, head from base_back, arms and legs from turn_back. Side (facing right; left mirrored): body = base_side.prev.png (the arm removed; legs cut away below the belt), arm cut from part_side_arm, legs from part_side_legs (split far / near and at the knee). Scale: 665 px per metre — the father is 1.17 × grandma's height in her drawn scale (she is 896 px at 747), so the family keeps its proportions. Stride 0.82 m: 2 × 400 px × sin 24° over 60 % of the cycle. Build: python3 tools/art/rig_build.py father. Arms re-cut wider (the first front/back polygons sliced the forearms); the side arm part was painted 2.3× too big and is fitted onto the rig pose by tools/art/place.gd (part_side_arm_fit.png). Hybrid poses: sit (sofa, facing the camera, 1.30 m seated), sit_side (eating at a table end, chair erased), repair (kneeling with a screwdriver: board repair tasks and the workshop's tools activity). Side view rebuilt (user review 2026-11-20: the legs were cut from a mid-stride painting and left jeans fragments swinging at the hip; the side body still had the hand painted on): from the straight profile — side_noarm (body without the arm), one whole straight leg (side_leg, fitted by place.gd) for both legs, split at the knee, and a whole arm (side_arm) — all mirrored to face right (tools/art/mirror.gd). Walk from straight legs: stance −24°→+24° linear, swing with the knee flexing to 45°. Stride 0.99 m from the leg length. Workshop 'tools': a standing bench pose (bench_back.png), not the kneeling repair pose. Every view at one scale (665). Repair: a two-legged kneeling profile (repair_side.png; the first repair pose had a third leg). It came out 1.57× larger than his other paintings (head 234 px against 149), so its view is drawn at 1044 px per metre to match his size.",
